{"id": "M2-c323", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Definition 1.1: what does the envelope theorem state?", "back": "Under stated hypotheses, the derivative of the value function V at a equals the partial derivative f_a of the objective at the optimal choice x_hat(a), with the choice held at its optimum and not differentiated."}
{"id": "M2-c324", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Lemma 1.2: what is the first-order condition at an interior maximizer?", "back": "Hypothesis: phi is differentiable on an open interval J and x_hat in J maximizes phi on J. Then phi'(x_hat) = 0. A nonzero derivative would give a larger value at a small step on one side.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c325", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Theorem 1.3 (envelope theorem, one choice variable): what does it state?", "back": "Hypotheses: (H1) f is continuously differentiable on J x I; (H2) for each a in I, x_hat(a) in J maximizes f(., a) on J; (H3) x_hat is differentiable on I. Then V(a) = f(x_hat(a), a) is differentiable on I, and V'(a) = f_a(x_hat(a), a).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c326", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Remark 1.4: where does hypothesis (H3) come from?", "back": "(H3) holds locally, on an interval around a0, when f is twice continuously differentiable, x_hat(a) maximizes f(., a) for a near a0 (H2), f_x = 0 and f_xx is nonzero at x_hat(a0), and x_hat is continuous at a0 (Remark 1.4). The argument applies Theorem 1.3 of Lesson 21 to G(a, x) = f_x(x, a), under its Assumption 1.2 (the intermediate value theorem) and Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem), both assumed there and not proved."}
{"id": "M2-c327", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Example 1.5: what is the value function of f(x, a) = a ln(1 + x) - x, and what is V'(a)?", "back": "The maximizer is x_hat(a) = a - 1 and V(a) = a ln a - (a - 1). Under Assumption 1.1 (the derivative of ln a is 1/a), V'(a) = ln a. The envelope formula f_a(x_hat, a) = ln(1 + x_hat) = ln a agrees.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c328", "lesson": "L22", "front": "In Lab 1 part A, do the central differences of V match ln a?", "back": "Yes. At a = 1.5, 2, 3 and 4 the central differences are 0.40546511, 0.69314718, 1.09861229 and 1.38629436, which equal ln a to eight decimals.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, part A (golden-section search on [0, 10], step 1e-4)"}
{"id": "M2-c329", "lesson": "L22", "front": "In Lab 1 part B, what do the central differences of V give for maximizing xy subject to x + y = a?", "back": "They give 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 at a = 1, 2 and 3, which agree with a/2 to within 5.4e-12. The naive partial of xy in a at fixed x and y prints 0.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, part B"}
{"id": "M2-c330", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Example 2.2: what is V'(a) when maximizing xy on the line x + y = a?", "back": "V(a) = a^2/4, so V'(a) = a/2, with maximizer x_hat(a) = a/2 and y*(a) = a/2. The naive partial of xy in a is zero and misses the term g_y c_a = a/2 (Proposition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c331", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Proposition 2.1 (constraint solved for one variable): what is V'(a)?", "back": "Hypotheses: g is continuously differentiable on an open set U in R^3 containing the points (x, c(x, a), a) for (x, a) in J x I; c is continuously differentiable on J x I; F(x, a) = g(x, c(x, a), a) satisfies (H1) to (H3) of Theorem 1.3 on J x I, with maximizer x_hat(a); and y*(a) = c(x_hat(a), a). Then V(a) = F(x_hat(a), a) and V'(a) = g_y(x_hat, y*, a) c_a(x_hat, a) + g_a(x_hat, y*, a).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c332", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Example 2.3: what is the value function of max over x in [0, 1] of ax, and is it differentiable at a = 0?", "back": "V(a) = a for a > 0, V(a) = 0 for a < 0, and V(0) = 0. The one-sided derivatives at 0 are 1 and 0, so V is not differentiable at 0.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c334", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Does the envelope theorem need the maximizer to be unique and interior?", "back": "Uniqueness is not assumed. Interiority is: (H2) puts x_hat(a) in the open interval J, and Example 2.3 (closed domain [0, 1]) is not covered. At a = 0 every point of [0, 1] is a maximizer, and V has different one-sided derivatives there (Example 2.3). (Misconception 2)", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c335", "lesson": "L22", "front": "When a constraint depends on a, is the derivative of the value the partial of the objective in a?", "back": "No. For the objective xy the partial in a is 0, while the true derivative is a/2. Proposition 2.1 shows the missing term g_y c_a (Misconception 1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c336", "lesson": "L22", "front": "Does a small error in the optimal choice ruin the derivative of the value?", "back": "Not in Lab 1. Part A prints x_hat with an error of about 10^-8, and the central difference of V still matches ln a to the printed digits (Misconception 3).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, part A"}
{"id": "M2-c337", "lesson": "L22", "front": "What are the value function and a maximizer?", "back": "For an open interval J of choices x and an open interval I of parameters a, with f on J x I, the value function is V(a) = max over x in J of f(x, a) whenever the maximum exists. A maximizer is a point x_hat(a) in J with f(x_hat(a), a) = V(a)."}
{"id": "M2-c338", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Definition 1.1: what is the price of a bond as a function of its yield?", "back": "For y > -1 with annual compounding, P(y) = sum over t from 1 to N of c_t (1 + y)^(-t), where c_t are the cash flows in cents at the ends of years 1 to N. A yield shift of 0.01 is 100 basis points."}
{"id": "M2-c339", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Proposition 2.1: what are the first and second derivatives of the price, and their signs?", "back": "P'(y) = -sum over t of t c_t (1 + y)^(-t-1) and P''(y) = sum over t of t(t+1) c_t (1 + y)^(-t-2). If every c_t is positive, then P'(y) < 0 and P''(y) > 0 for every y > -1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c340", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Proposition 2.2 (price at face when the yield equals the coupon rate): what is the price?", "back": "Hypotheses: N >= 1, face value F > 0, a coupon rate r > -1, coupons rF at each year t < N, and the face F added to the last coupon. If the yield equals r, the price equals F. The proof uses the geometric series with v = 1/(1 + r) and 1 - v = rv.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c341", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the unrounded price of the SIMULATED bond at a 4 percent yield?", "back": "108110.895779 cents. The bond is SIMULATED: face 100000 cents, a 5000-cent coupon for ten years, with the face repaid at year 10.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond)"}
{"id": "M2-c342", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what is P'(y0) for the SIMULATED bond at y0 = 0.04?", "back": "The closed form is -851466.739529, and the central difference agrees to 1.6e-03.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond)"}
{"id": "M2-c343", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what is P''(y0) for the SIMULATED bond at y0 = 0.04?", "back": "The closed form is 8376648.511919, and the central difference differs from it by 1.9.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond)"}
{"id": "M2-c345", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the first-order error at a shift of +0.01?", "back": "4.037716e+02 cents, the difference between the price and the first-order expansion (unrounded values).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond, unrounded values)"}
{"id": "M2-c346", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what are the first-order error ratios as the shift halves?", "back": "3.9279 and 3.9634, for shifts from 0.01 to 0.0025. They approach 4, but Lab 1 establishes this for these shifts only.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (three shifts)"}
{"id": "M2-c347", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Are the preview numbers -P'/P and P''/P defined?", "back": "No. They are printed only, and no claim is made about their use."}
{"id": "M2-c348", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Theorem 3.1 (second-order expansion of the price): what is the remainder bound?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). Hypotheses: y0 > -1 and y0 + Delta > -1. Then P(y0 + Delta) = P_2(Delta) + R(Delta), where P_2 is the second-order Taylor polynomial at y0, and |R(Delta)| <= (1/6) M |Delta|^3 with M the maximum of |P'''| on the closed interval between y0 and y0 + Delta.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c349", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Proposition 3.2: what is the sign of the remainder for positive cash flows?", "back": "Hypotheses: y0 > -1. Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). If every c_t is positive, R(Delta) has the sign of -Delta for every nonzero Delta with y0 + Delta > -1. So R is negative for a positive shift and positive for a negative shift. The proof uses P''' < 0.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c350", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Proposition 3.3: is the first-order expansion below the price for positive cash flows?", "back": "Yes. Hypotheses: y0 > -1. Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). If every c_t is positive, then P(y0 + Delta) - P_1(Delta) >= 0 for every Delta with y0 + Delta > -1. The difference is (1/2) P''(theta) Delta^2 for some theta between y0 and y0 + Delta.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c351", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Does the remainder bound of Theorem 3.1 give the size of the error?", "back": "Not as computed. Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6, the bound depends on the third derivative over the whole interval between the two yields, and no value of the bound is computed. Proposition 3.3 is an inequality and does not give the size of the error (Remark 3.4)."}
{"id": "M2-c352", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Is the second-order expansion of the price exact?", "back": "No. The remainder is not zero, and in Lab 1 the exact price at a shift of +0.01 differs from the second-order expansion by -15 cents (Misconception 1).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond)"}
{"id": "M2-c353", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Does a bond whose yield equals its coupon rate have a price different from face value?", "back": "No. Proposition 2.2 gives a price equal to face at that yield (Misconception 2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c354", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Does a higher yield always raise the price of a bond with positive cash flows?", "back": "No. Proposition 2.1 gives P'(y) < 0 when every cash flow is positive, so the price falls as the yield rises (Misconception 3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c355", "lesson": "L23", "front": "Does the expansion apply to a bond whose cash flows move with the yield?", "back": "Not as written. The expansion assumes that the cash flows do not depend on the yield, and the derivatives of Proposition 2.1 do not apply to a bond with a call or prepayment option as written (Section 4)."}
{"id": "M2-c357", "lesson": "L23", "front": "How is the bond price rounded to cents?", "back": "The price and the Taylor polynomials are exact rational numbers, computed from the float yields with Fraction. The two sensitivities P'(y0) and P''(y0) are floats, each rounded once to the nearest double, so the Taylor terms are exact given those floats. The price is rounded to an integer number of cents once, at the last step, with halves to even (Section 4)."}
{"id": "M2-c382", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what integer price in cents does the unrounded price at a 4 percent yield round to?", "back": "108111 cents, the unrounded price 108110.895779 rounded to an integer number of cents.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond)"}
{"id": "M2-c383", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the price of the SIMULATED bond at a shift of -0.01 from the base yield 0.04?", "back": "117060 cents.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond)"}
{"id": "M2-c384", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the second-order error at a shift of +0.01?", "back": "-1.506081e+01 cents, the remainder of the second-order expansion at that shift (unrounded values).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond, unrounded values)"}
{"id": "M2-c385", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what are the second-order error ratios as the shift halves?", "back": "7.8807 and 7.9396, for shifts from 0.01 to 0.0025. They approach 8, but Lab 1 establishes this for these shifts only.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (three shifts)"}
{"id": "M2-c386", "lesson": "L23", "front": "What does it mean for a bond to be at par?", "back": "A bond priced at its face value is said to be at par (Section 1)."}
{"id": "M2-c358", "lesson": "L24", "front": "What is the backward difference quotient of f at x with step h > 0?", "back": "D-_h f(x) = (f(x) - f(x - h))/h, which uses the values of f at x and at x - h (Section 1)."}
{"id": "M2-c359", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Theorem 2.1: what are the quotients of f(x) = |x| at 0, and does f have a derivative there?", "back": "For every step h > 0, the forward quotient is 1, the backward quotient is -1 and the central quotient is 0. The one-sided limits differ, so f has no derivative at 0.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c360", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Proposition 3.1 (truncation of the forward quotient): what is the bound?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). Hypotheses: f is twice differentiable on an open interval containing [x, x + h], and f'' is bounded on [x, x + h] by M_2. Then |D+_h f(x) - f'(x)| <= (1/2) M_2 h. The proof uses the Lagrange form of Taylor's theorem of order one (Lesson 8).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c361", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Proposition 3.2 (truncation of the central quotient): what is the bound?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). Hypotheses: f is three times differentiable on an open interval containing [x - h, x + h], and f''' is bounded on [x - h, x + h] by M_3. Then |D0_h f(x) - f'(x)| <= (1/6) M_3 h^2.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c362", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Definition 4.1 (the error hypothesis): what does it assume about the computed value of f?", "back": "That the computed value f-tilde satisfies |f-tilde(t) - f(t)| <= delta for every point t at which the quotient is evaluated, for a stated delta > 0. It counts only the error in evaluating f, not the rounding of the subtraction and the division."}
{"id": "M2-c363", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Proposition 4.2: what does the computed forward quotient satisfy under Definition 4.1?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). Hypotheses: f and x satisfy Definition 4.1, M_2 > 0, and H is the set of steps h > 0 for which f is twice differentiable on an open interval containing [x, x + h] and |f''| <= M_2 on [x, x + h]. For every h in H, the error of the computed forward quotient is at most (1/2) M_2 h + 2 delta/h. The bound function g(h) = (1/2) M_2 h + 2 delta/h is minimized over all h > 0 at h* = 2 sqrt(delta/M_2), where it equals 2 sqrt(M_2 delta). If h* is in H, the bound at h* is this minimum.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c364", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Proposition 4.3: what does the computed central quotient satisfy under Definition 4.1?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (the extreme value theorem). Hypotheses: f and x satisfy Definition 4.1, M_3 > 0, and H is the set of steps h > 0 for which f is three times differentiable on an open interval containing [x - h, x + h] and |f'''| <= M_3 on [x - h, x + h]. For every h in H, the error of the computed central quotient is at most (1/6) M_3 h^2 + delta/h. The bound function g(h) = (1/6) M_3 h^2 + delta/h is minimized over all h > 0 at h* = (3 delta/M_3)^(1/3). If h* is in H, the bound at h* is this minimum.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c365", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Remark 4.4: is the bound of Proposition 4.2 the error at its minimizing step?", "back": "No. Proposition 4.2 gives an upper bound at every step in H. The bound is guaranteed at h* only if h* is in H, that is, only if f is twice differentiable on an open interval containing [x, x + h*] and |f''| <= M_2 on [x, x + h*]. When h* is not in H, Proposition 4.2 says nothing about the error at h*. Truncation and round-off errors can have opposite signs, so a computed quotient's error can be smaller than its truncation error alone (Lab 1 Part B at h = 10^-8). Lab 1 does not measure delta or M_2."}
{"id": "M2-c367", "lesson": "L24", "front": "In Lab 1 part B, how does the forward error of exp at 1 change as the step falls from 1e-01 to 1e-05?", "back": "It falls from 1.406e-01 at h = 1e-01 to 1.359e-05 at h = 1e-05, close to (1/2) e h, which is the truncation term.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, part B (decade steps)"}
{"id": "M2-c368", "lesson": "L24", "front": "In Lab 1 part B, how does the forward error behave for steps below 1e-08?", "back": "It rises: it is 3.263e-05 at h = 1e-11 and 3.903e-01 at h = 1e-15, where round-off dominates.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, part B (decade steps)"}
{"id": "M2-c370", "lesson": "L24", "front": "In the Lab 1 model with assumed delta = 2.22 times 10^-16 and M_2 and M_3 approximately e, what minimizing steps do Propositions 4.2 and 4.3 give for exp at 1?", "back": "About 1.8 times 10^-8 for the forward quotient, from 2 sqrt(delta/e), and about 6.3 times 10^-6 for the central quotient, from (3 delta/e)^(1/3), with delta = 2.22 times 10^-16 assumed. M_2 and M_3 are taken as approximately e, from the derivative of exp. None of delta, M_2 or M_3 is measured, and the lab prints neither minimizer.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 Part B prediction (assumed delta; M_2 and M_3 approximately e; not measured)"}
{"id": "M2-c371", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Under Proposition 4.2, does the bound grow as the step falls below its minimizer?", "back": "Yes. The bound (1/2) M_2 h + 2 delta/h increases as h falls below h* = 2 sqrt(delta/M_2), because the term 2 delta/h grows faster than the term (1/2) M_2 h falls (Misconception 1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c372", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Does the central quotient give the derivative of |x| at 0?", "back": "No. D0_h f(0) = 0 for every h, which averages the one-sided slopes -1 and 1. The derivative of |x| at 0 does not exist (Theorem 2.1, Misconception 2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c373", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Does a finite-difference value that does not change with the step prove differentiability?", "back": "No. The forward quotient of |x| at 0 is 1 at every step, and |x| has no derivative there (Theorem 2.1, Misconception 3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c375", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Where does the round-off term 2 delta/h come from?", "back": "From the cancellation in the computed numerator f-tilde(x + h) - f-tilde(x), whose error is at most 2 delta (Proposition 4.2 proof, Section 4; Section 5 bullet on cancellation).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c387", "lesson": "L24", "front": "What is cancellation in a finite-difference quotient?", "back": "Subtracting two nearly equal computed values loses digits. In the forward quotient the numerator f~(x + h) - f~(x) is such a difference, and its error produces the round-off term 2 delta/h (Section 4)."}
{"id": "M2-c404", "lesson": "L22", "front": "What does Assumption 1.1 of Lesson 22 state?", "back": "For a > 0, the derivative of ln a is 1/a. This page assumes it and does not prove it; Example 1.5 uses it."}
{"id": "M2-c405", "lesson": "L24", "front": "What does Assumption 4.1 state?", "back": "For every real x, the derivative of e^x is e^x. This page assumes it and does not prove it; the value e at x0 = 1 relies on it."}
{"id": "M2-c407", "lesson": "L23", "front": "In Lab 1, what price does the SIMULATED bond print at the yield 0.05?", "back": "100000 cents, the face value. Proposition 2.2 predicts this, because the coupon rate of 5 percent equals the yield.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED bond, yield shift +0.01)"}
{"id": "M2-c410", "lesson": "L24", "front": "Definition 4.1: what are the computed forward and central quotients?", "back": "With f~ the computed value of f, the computed forward quotient is (f~(x + h) - f~(x))/h and the computed central quotient is (f~(x + h) - f~(x - h))/(2h). The tilde marks evaluation by f~, not the exact f (Definition 4.1)."}
